.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command primarily creates and processes certificate requests (CSRs)
in PKCS#10 format.
It can additionally create self-signed certificates for use as root CAs
for example.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The CSR input file format to use; by default PEM is tried first.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -outform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The output format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.Pp
The data is a PKCS#10 object.
.It Sy -cipher Em name
Specify the cipher to be used for encrypting the private key.
If no cipher is specified, AES-256-CBC will be used by default.
You can override this by providing any valid OpenSSL cipher name.
.It Sy -in Em filename
This specifies the input filename to read a request from.
This defaults to standard input unless
.Sy -x509
or
.Sy -CA
is specified.
A request is only read if the creation options
.Pf ( Sy -new
or
.Sy -newkey
or
.Sy -precert )
are not specified.
.It Sy -sigopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during sign operations.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific.
.It Sy -vfyopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during verify operations.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The password source for private key and certificate input.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -passout Em arg
The password source for the output file.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies the output filename to write to or standard output by
default.
.It Sy -text
Prints out the certificate request in text form.
.It Sy -subject
Prints out the certificate request subject (or certificate subject if
.Sy -x509
is in use).
.It Sy -pubkey
Prints out the public key.
.It Sy -noout
This option prevents output of the encoded version of the certificate
request.
.It Sy -modulus
Prints out the value of the modulus of the public key contained in the
request.
.It Sy -verify
Verifies the self-signature on the request.
If the verification fails, the program will immediately exit, i.e.
further option processing (e.g.
.Sy -text )
is skipped.
.It Sy -new
This option generates a new certificate request.
It will prompt the user for the relevant field values.
The actual fields prompted for and their maximum and minimum sizes are
specified in the configuration file and any requested extensions.
.Pp
If the
.Sy -key
option is not given it will generate a new private key using information
specified in the configuration file or given with the
.Sy -newkey
and
.Sy -pkeyopt
options, else by default an RSA key with 2048 bits length.
.It Sy -newkey Em arg
This option is used to generate a new private key unless
.Sy -key
is given.
It is subsequently used as if it was given using the
.Sy -key
option.
.Pp
This option implies the
.Sy -new
flag to create a new certificate request or a new certificate in case
.Sy -x509
is used.
.Pp
The argument takes one of several forms.
.Pp
.Pf [ Sy rsa: Ns ] Ns Em nbits
generates an RSA key
.Em nbits
in size.
If
.Em nbits
is omitted, i.e.,
.Sy -newkey
.Sy rsa
is specified, the default key size specified in the configuration file
with the
.Sy default_bits
option is used if present, else 2048.
.Pp
All other algorithms support the
.Sy -newkey
.Em algname Ns : Ns Em file
form, where
.Em file
is an algorithm parameter file, created with
.Qo Li openssl genpkey -genparam Qc
or an X.509 certificate for a key with appropriate algorithm.
.Pp
.Sy param: Ns Em file
generates a key using the parameter file or certificate
.Em file ,
the algorithm is determined by the parameters.
.Pp
.Em algname Ns [: Ns Em file ]
generates a key using the given algorithm
.Em algname .
If a parameter file
.Em file
is given then the parameters specified there are used, where the
algorithm parameters must match
.Em algname .
If algorithm parameters are not given, any necessary parameters should
be specified via the
.Sy -pkeyopt
option.
.Pp
.Sy dsa: Ns Em filename
generates a DSA key using the parameters in the file
.Em filename .
.Sy ec: Ns Em filename
generates EC key (usable both with ECDSA or ECDH algorithms),
.Sy gost2001: Ns Em filename
generates GOST R 34.10-2001 key (requires
.Sy gost
engine configured in the configuration file).
If just
.Sy gost2001
is specified a parameter set should be specified by
.Sy -pkeyopt
.Em paramset:X
.It Sy -pkeyopt Em opt Ns : Ns Em value
Set the public key algorithm option
.Em opt
to
.Em value .
The precise set of options supported depends on the public key algorithm
used and its implementation.
See
.Xr openssl-genpkey 1
for more details.
.It Sy -key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This option provides the private key for signing a new certificate or
certificate request.
Unless
.Sy -in
is given, the corresponding public key is placed in the new certificate
or certificate request, resulting in a self-signature.
.Pp
For certificate signing this option is overridden by the
.Sy -CA
option.
.Pp
This option also accepts PKCS#8 format private keys for PEM format
files.
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The format of the private key; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -keyout Em filename
This gives the filename to write any private key to that has been newly
created or read from
.Sy -key .
If neither the
.Sy -keyout
option nor the
.Sy -key
option are given then the filename specified in the configuration file
with the
.Sy default_keyfile
option is used, if present.
Thus, if you want to write the private key and the
.Sy -key
option is provided, you should provide the
.Sy -keyout
option explicitly.
If a new key is generated and no filename is specified the key is
written to standard output.
.It Sy -noenc
If this option is specified then if a private key is created it will not
be encrypted.
.It Sy -nodes
This option is deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0; use
.Sy -noenc
instead.
.It Sy - Ns Em digest
This specifies the message digest to sign the request.
Any digest supported by the OpenSSL
.Sy dgst
command can be used.
This overrides the digest algorithm specified in the configuration file.
.Pp
Some public key algorithms may override this choice.
For instance, DSA signatures always use SHA1, GOST R 34.10 signatures
always use GOST R 34.11-94
.Pf ( Sy -md_gost94 Ns ),
Ed25519 and Ed448 never use any digest.
.It Sy -config Em filename
This allows an alternative configuration file to be specified.
Optional; for a description of the default value, see
.Xr openssl 1 .
.It Sy -section Em name
Specifies the name of the section to use; the default is
.Sy req .
.It Sy -subj Em arg
Sets subject name for new request or supersedes the subject name when
processing a certificate request.
.Pp
The arg must be formatted as
.Qo Li /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... Qc .
Special characters may be escaped by
.Qo Li \e Qc
(backslash), whitespace is retained.
Empty values are permitted, but the corresponding type will not be
included in the request.
Giving a single
.Qo Li / Qc
will lead to an empty sequence of RDNs (a NULL-DN). Multi-valued RDNs
can be formed by placing a
.Qo Li + Qc
character instead of a
.Qo Li / Qc
between the AttributeValueAssertions (AVAs) that specify the members of
the set.
Example:
.Pp
.Qo Li /DC=org/DC=OpenSSL/DC=users/UID=123456+CN=John Doe Qc
.It Sy -multivalue-rdn
This option has been deprecated and has no effect.
.It Sy -x509
This option outputs a certificate instead of a certificate request.
This is typically used to generate test certificates.
It is implied by the
.Sy -CA
option.
.Pp
This option implies the
.Sy -new
flag if
.Sy -in
is not given.
.Pp
If an existing request is specified with the
.Sy -in
option, it is converted to a certificate; otherwise a request is created
from scratch.
.Pp
Unless specified using the
.Sy -set_serial
option, a large random number will be used for the serial number.
.Pp
Unless the
.Sy -copy_extensions
option is used, X.509 extensions are not copied from any provided
request input file.
.Pp
X.509 extensions to be added can be specified in the configuration file,
possibly using the
.Sy -config
and
.Sy -extensions
options, and/or using the
.Sy -addext
option.
.Pp
Unless
.Sy -x509v1
is given, generated certificates bear X.509 version 3. Unless specified
otherwise, key identifier extensions are included as described in
.Xr x509v3_config 5 .
.It Sy -x509v1
Request generation of certificates with X.509 version 1. This implies
.Sy -x509 .
If X.509 extensions are given, anyway X.509 version 3 is set.
.It Sy -CA Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Specifies the "CA" certificate to be used for signing a new certificate
and implies use of
.Sy -x509 .
When present, this behaves like a "micro CA" as follows: The subject
name of the "CA" certificate is placed as issuer name in the new
certificate, which is then signed using the "CA" key given as specified
below.
.It Sy -CAkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Sets the "CA" private key to sign a certificate with.
The private key must match the public key of the certificate given with
.Sy -CA .
If this option is not provided then the key must be present in the
.Sy -CA
input.
.It Sy -not_before Em date
When
.Sy -x509
is in use this allows the start date to be explicitly set, otherwise it
is ignored.
The format of
.Em date
is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime structure), or
YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.It Sy -not_after Em date
When
.Sy -x509
is in use this allows the expiry date to be explicitly set, otherwise it
is ignored.
The format of
.Em date
is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime structure), or
YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.Pp
This overrides the
.Sy -days
option.
.It Sy -days Em n
When
.Sy -x509
is in use this specifies the number of days from today to certify the
certificate for, otherwise it is ignored.
.Em n
should be a positive integer.
The default is 30 days.
.Pp
Regardless of the option
.Sy -not_before ,
the days are always counted from today.
When used together with the option
.Sy -not_after ,
the explicit expiry date takes precedence.
.It Sy -set_serial Em n
Serial number to use when outputting a self-signed certificate.
This may be specified as a decimal value or a hex value if preceded by
.Qo Li 0x Qc .
If not given, a large random number will be used.
.It Sy -copy_extensions Em arg
Determines how X.509 extensions in certificate requests should be
handled when
.Sy -x509
is in use.
If
.Em arg
is
.Sy none
or this option is not present then extensions are ignored.
If
.Em arg
is
.Sy copy
or
.Sy copyall
then all extensions in the request are copied to the certificate.
.Pp
The main use of this option is to allow a certificate request to supply
values for certain extensions such as subjectAltName.
.It Sy -extensions Em section Ns , Sy -reqexts Em section
Can be used to override the name of the configuration file section from
which X.509 extensions are included in the certificate (when
.Sy -x509
is in use) or certificate request.
This allows several different sections to be used in the same
configuration file to specify requests for a variety of purposes.
.It Sy -addext Em ext
Add a specific extension to the certificate (if
.Sy -x509
is in use) or certificate request.
The argument must have the form of a
.Qo Li key=value Qc
pair as it would appear in a config file.
.Pp
If an extension is added using this option that has the same OID as one
defined in the extension section of the config file, it overrides that
one.
.Pp
This option can be given multiple times.
Doing so, the same key most not be given more than once.
.It Sy -precert
A poison extension will be added to the certificate, making it a
"pre-certificate" (see RFC6962). This can be submitted to Certificate
Transparency logs in order to obtain signed certificate timestamps
(SCTs).
These SCTs can then be embedded into the pre-certificate as an
extension, before removing the poison and signing the certificate.
.Pp
This implies the
.Sy -new
flag.
.It Sy -utf8
This option causes field values to be interpreted as UTF8 strings, by
default they are interpreted as ASCII. This means that the field values,
whether prompted from a terminal or obtained from a configuration file,
must be valid UTF8 strings.
.It Sy -reqopt Em option
Customise the printing format used with
.Sy -text .
The
.Em option
argument can be a single option or multiple options separated by commas.
.Pp
See discussion of the
.Sy -certopt
parameter in the
.Xr openssl-x509 1
command.
.It Sy -newhdr
Adds the word
.Sy NEW
to the PEM file header and footer lines on the outputted request.
Some software (Netscape certificate server) and some CAs need this.
.It Sy -batch
Non-interactive mode.
.It Sy -verbose
Print extra details about the operations being performed.
.It Sy -quiet
Print fewer details about the operations being performed, which may be
handy during batch scripts or pipelines (specifically "progress dots"
during key generation are suppressed).
.It Sy -keygen_engine Em id
Specifies an engine (by its unique
.Em id
string) which would be used for key generation operations.
.It Sy -nameopt Em option
This specifies how the subject or issuer names are displayed.
See
.Xr openssl-namedisplay-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE FORMAT
The configuration options are specified in the
.Sy req
section of the configuration file.
An alternate name be specified by using the
.Sy -section
option.
As with all configuration files, if no value is specified in the
specific section then the initial unnamed or
.Sy default
section is searched too.
.Pp
The options available are described in detail below.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy input_password Ns , Sy output_password
The passwords for the input private key file (if present) and the output
private key file (if one will be created).
The command line options
.Sy passin
and
.Sy passout
override the configuration file values.
.It Sy default_bits
Specifies the default key size in bits.
.Pp
This option is used in conjunction with the
.Sy -new
option to generate a new key.
It can be overridden by specifying an explicit key size in the
.Sy -newkey
option.
The smallest accepted key size is 512 bits.
If no key size is specified then 2048 bits is used.
.It Sy default_keyfile
This is the default filename to write a private key to.
If not specified the key is written to standard output.
This can be overridden by the
.Sy -keyout
option.
.It Sy oid_file
This specifies a file containing additional
.Sy OBJECT IDENTIFIERS .
Each line of the file should consist of the numerical form of the object
identifier followed by whitespace then the short name followed by
whitespace and finally the long name.
.It Sy oid_section
This specifies a section in the configuration file containing extra
object identifiers.
Each line should consist of the short name of the object identifier
followed by
.Sy =
and the numerical form.
The short and long names are the same when this option is used.
.It Sy RANDFILE
At startup the specified file is loaded into the random number
generator, and at exit 256 bytes will be written to it.
It is used for private key generation.
.It Sy encrypt_key
If this is set to
.Sy no
then if a private key is generated it is
.Sy not
encrypted.
This is equivalent to the
.Sy -noenc
command line option.
For compatibility
.Sy encrypt_rsa_key
is an equivalent option.
.It Sy default_md
This option specifies the digest algorithm to use.
Any digest supported by the OpenSSL
.Sy dgst
command can be used.
This option can be overridden on the command line.
Certain signing algorithms (i.e.
Ed25519 and Ed448) will ignore any digest that has been set.
.It Sy string_mask
This option masks out the use of certain string types in certain fields.
Most users will not need to change this option.
It can be set to several values:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy utf8only No - only UTF8Strings are used (this is the default value)
.It Sy pkix No - any string type except T61Strings
.It Sy nombstr No - any string type except BMPStrings and UTF8Strings
.It Sy default No - any kind of string type
.El
Note that
.Sy utf8only
is the PKIX recommendation in RFC2459 after 2003, and the default
.Sy string_mask ;
.Sy default
is not the default option.
The
.Sy nombstr
value is a workaround for some software that has problems with
variable-sized BMPStrings and UTF8Strings.
.It Sy req_extensions
This specifies the configuration file section containing a list of
extensions to add to the certificate request.
It can be overridden by the
.Sy -reqexts
(or
.Sy -extensions )
command line switch.
See the
.Xr x509v3_config 5
manual page for details of the extension section format.
.It Sy x509_extensions
This specifies the configuration file section containing a list of
extensions to add to certificate generated when
.Sy -x509
is in use.
It can be overridden by the
.Sy -extensions
command line switch.
.It Sy prompt
If set to the value
.Sy no
this disables prompting of certificate fields and just takes values from
the config file directly.
It also changes the expected format of the
.Sy distinguished_name
and
.Sy attributes
sections.
.It Sy utf8
If set to the value
.Sy yes
then field values to be interpreted as UTF8 strings, by default they are
interpreted as ASCII. This means that the field values, whether prompted
from a terminal or obtained from a configuration file, must be valid
UTF8 strings.
.It Sy attributes
This specifies the section containing any request attributes: its format
is the same as
.Sy distinguished_name .
Typically these may contain the challengePassword or unstructuredName
types.
They are currently ignored by OpenSSL's request signing utilities but
some CAs might want them.
.It Sy distinguished_name
This specifies the section containing the distinguished name fields to
prompt for when generating a certificate or certificate request.
The format is described in the next section.
.El
.Sh DISTINGUISHED NAME AND ATTRIBUTE SECTION FORMAT
There are two separate formats for the distinguished name and attribute
sections.
If the
.Sy prompt
option is set to
.Sy no
then these sections just consist of field names and values: for example,
.Bd -literal
 CN=My Name
 OU=My Organization
 emailAddress=someone@somewhere.org
.Ed
.Pp
This allows external programs (e.g.
GUI based) to generate a template file with all the field names and
values and just pass it to this command.
An example of this kind of configuration file is contained in the
.Sy EXAMPLES
section.
.Pp
Alternatively if the
.Sy prompt
option is absent or not set to
.Sy no
then the file contains field prompting information.
It consists of lines of the form:
.Bd -literal
 fieldName="prompt"
 fieldName_default="default field value"
 fieldName_min= 2
 fieldName_max= 4
.Ed
.Pp
"fieldName" is the field name being used, for example commonName (or
CN). The "prompt" string is used to ask the user to enter the relevant
details.
If the user enters nothing then the default value is used if no default
value is present then the field is omitted.
A field can still be omitted if a default value is present if the user
just enters the '.' character.
.Pp
The number of characters entered must be between the fieldName_min and
fieldName_max limits: there may be additional restrictions based on the
field being used (for example countryName can only ever be two
characters long and must fit in a PrintableString).
.Pp
Some fields (such as organizationName) can be used more than once in a
DN. This presents a problem because configuration files will not
recognize the same name occurring twice.
To avoid this problem if the fieldName contains some characters followed
by a full stop they will be ignored.
So for example a second organizationName can be input by calling it
"1.organizationName".
.Pp
The actual permitted field names are any object identifier short or long
names.
These are compiled into OpenSSL and include the usual values such as
commonName, countryName, localityName, organizationName,
organizationalUnitName, stateOrProvinceName.
Additionally emailAddress is included as well as name, surname,
givenName, initials, and dnQualifier.
.Pp
Additional object identifiers can be defined with the
.Sy oid_file
or
.Sy oid_section
options in the configuration file.
Any additional fields will be treated as though they were a
DirectoryString.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Examine and verify certificate request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -in req.pem -text -verify -noout
.Ed
.Pp
Specify the cipher to be used for encrypting the private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout privatekey.pem -out request.csr -cipher aes-256-cbc
.Ed
.Pp
Create a private key and then generate a certificate request from it:
.Bd -literal
 openssl genrsa -out key.pem 2048
 openssl req -new -key key.pem -out req.pem
.Ed
.Pp
The same but just using req:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout key.pem -out req.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a self-signed root certificate:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout key.pem -out req.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Create an SM2 private key and then generate a certificate request from
it:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ecparam -genkey -name SM2 -out sm2.key
 openssl req -new -key sm2.key -out sm2.csr -sm3 -sigopt "distid:1234567812345678"
.Ed
.Pp
Examine and verify an SM2 certificate request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -verify -in sm2.csr -sm3 -vfyopt "distid:1234567812345678"
.Ed
.Pp
Example of a file pointed to by the
.Sy oid_file
option:
.Bd -literal
 1.2.3.4        shortName       A longer Name
 1.2.3.6        otherName       Other longer Name
.Ed
.Pp
Example of a section pointed to by
.Sy oid_section
making use of variable expansion:
.Bd -literal
 testoid1=1.2.3.5
 testoid2=${testoid1}.6
.Ed
.Pp
Sample configuration file prompting for field values:
.Bd -literal
 [ req ]
 default_bits           = 2048
 default_keyfile        = privkey.pem
 distinguished_name     = req_distinguished_name
 attributes             = req_attributes
 req_extensions         = v3_ca

 dirstring_type = nombstr

 [ req_distinguished_name ]
 countryName                    = Country Name (2 letter code)
 countryName_default            = AU
 countryName_min                = 2
 countryName_max                = 2

 localityName                   = Locality Name (eg, city)

 organizationalUnitName         = Organizational Unit Name (eg, section)

 commonName                     = Common Name (eg, YOUR name)
 commonName_max                 = 64

 emailAddress                   = Email Address
 emailAddress_max               = 40

 [ req_attributes ]
 challengePassword              = A challenge password
 challengePassword_min          = 4
 challengePassword_max          = 20

 [ v3_ca ]

 subjectKeyIdentifier=hash
 authorityKeyIdentifier=keyid:always,issuer:always
 basicConstraints = critical, CA:true
.Ed
.Pp
Sample configuration containing all field values:
.Bd -literal
 [ req ]
 default_bits           = 2048
 default_keyfile        = keyfile.pem
 distinguished_name     = req_distinguished_name
 attributes             = req_attributes
 prompt                 = no
 output_password        = mypass

 [ req_distinguished_name ]
 C                      = GB
 ST                     = Test State or Province
 L                      = Test Locality
 O                      = Organization Name
 OU                     = Organizational Unit Name
 CN                     = Common Name
 emailAddress           = test@email.address

 [ req_attributes ]
 challengePassword              = A challenge password
.Ed
.Pp
Example of giving the most common attributes (subject and extensions) on
the command line:
.Bd -literal
 openssl req -new -subj "/C=GB/CN=foo" \e
                  -addext "subjectAltName = DNS:foo.co.uk" \e
                  -addext "certificatePolicies = 1.2.3.4" \e
                  -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout key.pem -out req.pem
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The certificate requests generated by
.Sy Xenroll
with MSIE have extensions added.
It includes the
.Sy keyUsage
extension which determines the type of key (signature only or general
purpose) and any additional OIDs entered by the script in an
.Sy extendedKeyUsage
extension.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The following messages are frequently asked about:
.Bd -literal
        Using configuration from /some/path/openssl.cnf
        Unable to load config info
.Ed
.Pp
This is followed some time later by:
.Bd -literal
        unable to find 'distinguished_name' in config
        problems making Certificate Request
.Ed
.Pp
The first error message is the clue: it can't find the configuration
file!
Certain operations (like examining a certificate request) don't need a
configuration file so its use isn't enforced.
Generation of certificates or requests however does need a configuration
file.
This could be regarded as a bug.
.Pp
Another puzzling message is this:
.Bd -literal
        Attributes:
            a0:00
.Ed
.Pp
this is displayed when no attributes are present and the request
includes the correct empty
.Sy SET OF
structure (the DER encoding of which is 0xa0 0x00). If you just see:
.Bd -literal
        Attributes:
.Ed
.Pp
then the
.Sy SET OF
is missing and the encoding is technically invalid (but it is
tolerated).
See the description of the command line option
.Sy -asn1-kludge
for more information.
.Sh BUGS
OpenSSL's handling of T61Strings (aka TeletexStrings) is broken: it
effectively treats them as ISO-8859-1 (Latin 1), Netscape and MSIE have
similar behaviour.
This can cause problems if you need characters that aren't available in
PrintableStrings and you don't want to or can't use BMPStrings.
.Pp
As a consequence of the T61String handling the only correct way to
represent accented characters in OpenSSL is to use a BMPString:
unfortunately Netscape currently chokes on these.
If you have to use accented characters with Netscape and MSIE then you
currently need to use the invalid T61String form.
.Pp
The current prompting is not very friendly.
It doesn't allow you to confirm what you've just entered.
Other things like extensions in certificate requests are statically
defined in the configuration file.
Some of these: like an email address in subjectAltName should be input
by the user.
